On war-torn land, hills and streams remain;
In capital spring, grass and trees run thick.
Grieving the times, e'en flowers shed tears;
Regretting parting, e'en birds break the heart.
Beacon fires have blazed three months long;
A word from home is worth a fortune in gold.
I scratch my head, growing thinner and paler,
Almost too thin for the pin of my hat.

